P("<b>1.4 Inquiry and conclusion.</b> A wireless link alone leaves exactly the unavailable fraction of updates lost (A loses 9.6, 38.2 and 67.2 of 96 readings). Store-and-forward at the <i>application</i> layer converts almost all of these into delayed deliveries (B delivers 99.1 %, 96.6 % and 94.0 % of the 96 updates within the shift, and 99.8 %, 99.2 % and 98.2 % once the few still-queued readings are synchronised later; only queue overflow is truly lost), but it exposes the characteristics that are specific to mobile computing: (1) <b>disconnected operation</b> — the app must keep working with no link; (2) <b>delay/staleness</b> — mean delay rises 2.4 → 9.5 → 17.4 min, worst case over an hour; (3) <b>finite local storage</b> — at 30 % availability 1.7 readings overflow the 20-item queue per shift and 4.1 are not yet synchronised; (4) <b>consistency</b> — ordering, duplicate suppression (idempotent uploads) and conflict resolution when several tablets edit the same work order; (5) <b>energy and resource limits</b> — retries drain the battery. None of these is solved by a better radio.")
