FIG("q3_block.png",16.5,"Figure 3.1 — Spread/despread chain; example of two users’ chips (N = 8) and the Walsh correlation matrix (N = 16).")
BL(["<b>Spreading:</b> user k’s bit b<sub>k</sub> ∈ {±1} is multiplied by its code c<sub>k</sub> ∈ {±1}<super>N</super>, giving N chips per bit (chip rate R<sub>c</sub> = N·R<sub>b</sub>). Walsh codes = rows of the Hadamard matrix; random codes = i.i.d. ±1 (seeded).",
"<b>Channel:</b> chips of all K users add at the base station, r = Σ A<sub>j</sub> b<sub>j</sub> c<sub>j</sub> + n, with AWGN set so that E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 10 dB (σ<super>2</super> = N / (2·10<super>1</super>) per chip). Asynchronous users are modelled by random integer chip delays τ<sub>j</sub> ∈ [1, N−1].",
"<b>Despreading and decision:</b> z<sub>k</sub> = (1/N) Σ<sub>n</sub> r[n] c<sub>k</sub>[n] over each bit period (aligned to user k’s timing); decision b̂<sub>k</sub> = sign(z<sub>k</sub>).",
"<b>Processing gain:</b> G<sub>p</sub> = R<sub>c</sub>/R<sub>b</sub> = N → 9.03 dB (N = 8), 12.04 dB (16), 18.06 dB (64). After despreading, the desired term is b<sub>k</sub>, each interferer contributes A<sub>j</sub> b<sub>j</sub> ρ<sub>jk</sub> with ρ<sub>jk</sub> = c<sub>j</sub>·c<sub>k</sub>/N; for random codes E[ρ²] = 1/N, so MAI variance ≈ (K−1)/N and BER ≈ Q(1/√((K−1)/N + N<sub>0</sub>/2E<sub>b</sub>)) (Gaussian approximation, Viterbi 1995)."])
P("<b>3.2 Predictions made before execution.</b> (i) Walsh, synchronous, equal power: ρ = 0 exactly ⇒ BER equals the single-user value Q(√(2E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>)) ≈ 4×10<super>−6</super> ⇒ zero errors in ~10<super>5</super> bits, flat in K. (ii) Random codes: BER rises with load K/N almost independently of N (≈ 0.06 at K/N = 0.5; ≈ 0.1 at K/N = 1). (iii) Walsh, synchronous, one user at 10× power: orthogonality is exact so I expect <i>still</i> ≈ 0 errors — the near-far problem needs some non-ideality to bite. I therefore added conditions (iv) Walsh with chip-asynchronous users (equal power) — orthogonality lost, BER similar to random codes — and (v) Walsh asynchronous <i>plus</i> one 10×-power user (amplitude √10) — worst case, error floor even at K = 2; (vi) random codes + 10× power.")
