P("<b>Prediction check.</b> (i) confirmed: 0 errors at every load (and 0 in the noise-free check). (ii) confirmed in shape: BER depends on load K/N, not on N, and matches the Gaussian theory column within sampling error (e.g. N = 64, K = 32: 0.072 simulated vs 0.085 theory; random codes at K = N reach 0.13–0.16). (iii) confirmed: <b>a 10× power user does nothing to perfectly orthogonal synchronous codes</b> — so ‘near-far’ is not caused by power alone. (iv) confirmed: asynchronous Walsh behaves like random codes (BER up to 0.125, many cells 0.03–0.13; it varies with the particular delay draw because Walsh cross-correlation at a shift ranges from 0 to 1 — worst case |ρ| = 1.0 in the table). (v) confirmed and strongest: with zero noise the same orthogonal code set gives 0 errors when synchronous but 6,963–8,945 errors in 60,000 bits (≈ 12–15 % BER) when asynchronous with a 10× user; at N = 8, K = 2 it already gives BER 0.044 vs 0.0001 with equal power.")
P("<b>3.4 Inquiry</b>",H2)
BL(["<b>Why do orthogonal codes still fail when users are unsynchronised or powers unequal?</b> Orthogonality is a property of the <i>zero-lag</i> inner product only: c<sub>j</sub>·c<sub>k</sub> = 0 for aligned codes, but for a delay of τ chips the correlation of Walsh codes is arbitrary (up to 1). Real uplinks are asynchronous (different propagation delays, multipath) so the residual ρ(τ) is non-zero; the MAI term is A<sub>j</sub>·ρ<sub>jk</sub>, i.e. it scales with the <i>interferer’s amplitude</i>. With unequal powers the product A·ρ of one strong user dwarfs the desired signal.",
"<b>Why is power control essential?</b> MAI is additive, so the SIR for user k is ≈ N / Σ<sub>j≠k</sub>(P<sub>j</sub>/P<sub>k</sub>) (plus noise). One 10×-power user counts as ten equal users: from the capacity estimate below, N = 64 supports ≈ 9 equal-power users at BER 10<super>−2</super>, so a single 10× user alone consumes the whole budget; with path-loss differences of 60–80 dB in a stadium/cell the weak users would be completely blocked. Closed-loop power control (fast, ~1 dB steps; IS-95/WCDMA ≈ 800–1,500 commands/s) forces equal received power, so capacity is bounded by ΣP/P = K−1 and MAI is fair.",
"<b>Simultaneous users supported</b> for my target <b>uncoded BER ≤ 10<super>−2</super> at E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 10 dB</b> (random/asynchronous codes, equal power): simulation gives K<sub>max</sub> = 2, 3 and 9 for N = 8, 16, 64; the Gaussian formula gives 2, 3, 9 and 18 for N = 128 — i.e. K<sub>max</sub> ≈ 0.14 N uncoded (≈ 0.5 + N/7). With rate-½ coding and soft decisions the required SIR drops by several dB and K<sub>max</sub> grows several-fold (not simulated). Perfect synchronous Walsh would support K = N = 8, 16, 64."])

# ============ Q4 ============
S.append(PageBreak()); P("Q4 — OFDMA: giving each user the subcarriers they can use best (6 marks)",H1)
P("<b>4.1 Subcarrier spacing, cyclic prefix and numerical orthogonality</b> (<font name='Courier'>q4.py</font>). Numerology (IEEE 802.11a/g-style, 20 MHz): N = 64 subcarriers, Δf = 20 MHz/64 = 312.5 kHz, useful symbol T<sub>u</sub> = 1/Δf = 3.2 µs, cyclic prefix T<sub>cp</sub> = 0.8 µs (¼ T<sub>u</sub>), symbol T<sub>s</sub> = 4 µs, so 250,000 OFDM symbols/s and 80 % time efficiency. <b>Relation:</b> subcarrier k is exp(j2πkΔf t); over T<sub>u</sub> the integral of exp(j2π(k−m)Δf t) is zero for k≠m only if Δf·T<sub>u</sub> is an integer — hence Δf = 1/T<sub>u</sub> (the minimum spacing that is orthogonal). <b>Why a cyclic prefix:</b> a multipath channel with delay spread τ<sub>max</sub> smears each symbol into the next (ISI) and destroys the integer-cycle property inside the FFT window (ICI). Copying the last T<sub>cp</sub> ≥ τ<sub>max</sub> of the symbol to its front makes the linear convolution look circular, so each subcarrier sees a single complex gain H<sub>k</sub> and one-tap equalisation works.")
o=q4["orth"]
P(f"<b>Numerical verification</b> (inner products of subcarriers k = 3, 4, 5 sampled at 64 points): the normalised Gram matrix is the identity; the largest off-diagonal magnitude is <b>{o['max_offdiag']:.1e}</b> (floating-point zero). If the receiver oscillator is off by 0.1 Δf, the leakage between neighbouring carriers is {o['leak_df_offset_0p1']:.3f} (−{-20*np.log10(o['leak_df_offset_0p1']):.0f} dB amplitude) and by 0.3 Δf it is {o['leak_df_offset_0p3']:.3f} — OFDMA is sensitive to frequency error.")
e=q4["evm_cp"]
P("<b>Cyclic-prefix test</b> (6-tap channel, 2,000 random QPSK symbols, one-tap equaliser): mean-square error vs CP length — "+", ".join(f"CP={k}: {v:.1e}" for k,v in e.items())+". The error vanishes (~10<super>−31</super>) once CP ≥ taps−1 = 5 samples, confirming the CP rule.")
FIG("q4_orth.png",12,"Figure 4.1 — Three orthogonal subcarriers (left) and the CP-length experiment (right).")
P("<b>4.2 Allocation designs and predictions.</b> Eight users, 64 subcarriers. Channels: frequency-selective Rayleigh, 16-tap exponential power-delay profile (decay constant 3 samples) transformed by 64-point FFT, fixed seed 42, <b>1,000 realisations</b>; mean SNR per user 24, 21, 18, 15, 12, 9, 6, 3 dB (<i>assumed</i> path-loss spread in the hall); rate per subcarrier = log<sub>2</sub>(1+SNR·|H<sub>k</sub>|²) bit/symbol (ideal adaptive modulation), throughput = bits × 250 kHz. Schemes: <b>(A) contiguous blocks</b> (8 adjacent subcarriers per user), <b>(B) interleaved round-robin</b> (subcarrier k → user k mod 8), <b>(C) channel-aware best-subcarrier</b> (each subcarrier to the user with the highest rate = max-sum), and two fair designs added for the design decision: <b>(D) max-min greedy</b> (repeatedly give the currently poorest user its best free subcarrier) and <b>(E) min-rate + max-sum</b> (phase 1: poorest user below R<sub>min</sub> takes its best free subcarrier until everyone has R<sub>min</sub>; phase 2: remaining subcarriers to the best user).")
FIG("q4_map.png",13,"Figure 4.2 — One channel realisation: which user owns which subcarrier under each scheme.")
P("<b>Predictions (before running):</b> sum throughput C ≫ B ≈ A (multiuser diversity); I also predicted B &gt; A in sum rate (frequency diversity). Jain’s index J = (Σx)²/(n·Σx²): A ≈ B ≈ 0.8 because the SNR spread, not the access rule, creates the unfairness; C worst (&lt; 0.4) because weak-SNR users almost never hold the best subcarrier; D ≈ 1.")
