FIG("q4_alloc.png",16.5,"Figure 4.3 — Per-user throughput (left, dashed = R_min), sum throughput and Jain index (middle), benefit of channel-aware allocation vs number of users (right).")
P("<b>Prediction check.</b> Confirmed: best-subcarrier gives 125.7 Mbps (+94 % over 64.7), and Jain collapses to 0.27 with users U6–U8 receiving 0.01, 0.00 and 0.00 Mbps — complete starvation. <b>Not confirmed:</b> I predicted interleaved &gt; contiguous in sum rate, but both give ≈ 64.7 Mbps; with a 16-tap channel the 8-subcarrier block (2.5 MHz) and the interleaved set are both random with respect to each user’s fades, so mean sum rate is identical. Interleaving’s real benefit is <i>reliability</i>: the weakest user falls below 2 Mbps in 17.5 % of realisations vs 35.8 % with contiguous blocks, and P(all users ≥ 2 Mbps) is 81 % vs 52 %, because frequency diversity averages out deep fades.")
P("<b>4.3 Inquiry</b>",H2)
BL(["<b>Why can maximising total throughput starve users?</b> Each subcarrier is given to the user with the highest rate there. Users with high mean SNR (U1: 24 dB) win almost every subcarrier since log<sub>2</sub>(1+SNR|H|²) is monotone in SNR; a 3 dB user wins only if all stronger users are simultaneously in a deep fade — practically never. The objective ‘sum rate’ contains no term for each user’s share, so the optimum is a corner solution (U1 77.6 Mbps, U2 34.1 vs U8 0.00).",
"<b>How does the number of users change the benefit?</b> With equal 12 dB mean SNR the gain of channel-aware over interleaved allocation is 0 % (1 user), 23.6 % (2), 41.6 % (4), 54.4 % (8), 64.8 % (16) and 72.5 % (32): multiuser diversity — the maximum of U independent Rayleigh gains grows ≈ ln U — with diminishing returns. With one user there is nothing to choose between. The cost is CSI feedback: U×64 channel reports per realisation."])
P("<b>4.4 Design decision — lecture hall with a guaranteed minimum rate.</b> <b>R<sub>min</sub> = 2 Mbps per student</b> (justification: a 720p live-lecture video stream needs ≈ 1.5–3 Mbps; 2 Mbps also supports slides + audio with margin; total demand 8×2 = 16 Mbps is only 25 % of the 64.7 Mbps ‘fair-blind’ capacity so the constraint is feasible). Results: contiguous and interleaved violate it for at least one student in 48 % and 19 % of realisations; max-sum violates it for 4.8 students on average; <b>min-rate + max-sum (E) meets R<sub>min</sub> for all eight users in 100 % of the 1,000 realisations</b> (the weakest user averages 2.33 Mbps) while keeping 114.2 Mbps = 91 % of the max-sum throughput. Max-min (D) is the choice if <i>equal</i> service is wanted (Jain 0.999, everyone ≥ 8.2 Mbps) but sacrifices 39 % of the sum throughput versus E (69.4 vs 114.2 Mbps). <b>Selected: (E)</b>, because the stated requirement is a floor, not equality. Caveats: needs per-subcarrier CSI feedback every coherence time, and a floor can only be guaranteed while U × R<sub>min</sub> is below capacity — admission control is needed for large halls.")

# ============ Q5 ============
S.append(PageBreak()); P("Q5 — SDMA: reusing the same channel in different directions (6 marks)",H1)
P("<b>5.1 Array-factor derivation.</b> N isotropic elements on the x-axis at positions x<sub>n</sub> = n·d, wavelength λ, plane wave from angle θ (from broadside). The phase of element n relative to element 0 is n·k·d·sinθ with k = 2π/λ. To steer to θ<sub>0</sub> each element is weighted by w<sub>n</sub> = exp(−j n k d sinθ<sub>0</sub>). Then AF(θ) = Σ<sub>n=0</sub><super>N−1</super> exp(j n ψ), ψ = k d (sinθ − sinθ<sub>0</sub>). Summing the geometric series: AF = (1 − e<super>jNψ</super>)/(1 − e<super>jψ</super>) = e<super>j(N−1)ψ/2</super>·sin(Nψ/2)/sin(ψ/2), so <b>|AF|/N = |sin(Nψ/2) / (N sin(ψ/2))|</b>. Consequences (d = λ/2): main lobe at ψ = 0; <b>nulls at Nψ/2 = mπ</b>, i.e. sinθ − sinθ<sub>0</sub> = 2m/N, so the first-null half-width at broadside is asin(2/N) and the null-to-null beamwidth ≈ 2·asin(2/N) ≈ 4/N rad; HPBW ≈ 0.886 λ/(N d cosθ<sub>0</sub>) = 101.5°/(N cosθ<sub>0</sub>) for d = λ/2 — the beam narrows ∝ 1/N and broadens by 1/cosθ<sub>0</sub> when steered off broadside; side lobes at |sin(Nψ/2)| = 1 give |AF|/N ≈ 1/(N sin(ψ/2)) → first side lobe ≈ −13.3 dB for large N (sinc limit 0.2172) <b>independent of N</b> for uniform weights; array gain = N (10log<sub>10</sub>N dB).")
P("<b>Predictions (before computing):</b> HPBW ≈ 51°, 25°, 12.7°, 6.3° for N = 2, 4, 8, 16; first side lobe ≈ −11 dB (N = 4) → −13.3 dB (large N); at SIR threshold 10 dB the minimum separation shrinks ≈ 1/N: ≈ 5–6° for N = 16, ≈ 11° for N = 8, ≈ 22° for N = 4; omni and the 120° sector cannot separate users in the same sector (SIR ≈ 0–3 dB).")
P("<b>5.2 SIR definition and computation</b> (<font name='Courier'>q5.py</font>). Two users A and B with equal power; the beam is steered at A (θ<sub>A</sub> = 0°, broadside). B is at angular separation Δ. The interference-to-signal leakage is |AF(Δ)|², so <b>SIR = −20·log<sub>10</sub>|AF(Δ)/N|</b> dB (equal for downlink leakage into B and, by reciprocity, uplink rejection of B). <b>Threshold: SIR ≥ 10 dB</b> (≈ QPSK with rate-½ coding; I also report 15 dB). Omni: SIR = 0 dB. 120° sector: 3GPP pattern attenuation min[12(Δ/65°)², 20 dB] (TR 25.996 / 36.814).")
