P("<b>Prediction check.</b> HPBW and nulls match theory (6.34° vs 6.34° for N = 16). Side lobe levels: −11.3 dB (N = 4), −12.8 dB (8), −13.15 dB (16) → −13.26 dB limit, as derived. At 10 dB the minimum separation is 22.1° (N = 4), 10.7° (N = 8), <b>5.3° (N = 16)</b>, 52.7° (N = 2); omni never separates, the 120° sector reaches only 10 dB at 60°. <b>Surprise:</b> with uniform weights the first side lobe (≈ −13 dB) means the 15 dB threshold is never met for N = 2, is met only at isolated angles (≥ 25°) for N = 4, and is guaranteed only beyond 11.9° (N = 16) and 24.7° (N = 8) — so the minimum separation depends on the SIR you demand, and amplitude tapering (Chebyshev/Hamming) would be needed to buy a lower side lobe at the price of a wider beam.")
P("<b>5.3 Inquiry — what limits SDMA in practice?</b>",H2)
mc=pd.read_csv(f"{DATA}/q5_spread.csv"); m16=mc[mc.N==16].pivot(index="sep",columns="sigma",values="P_SIR_ge10").reset_index(); m16.columns=["Separation (deg)","σ = 0° (ideal)","σ = 2°","σ = 5°"]
P("Monte-Carlo with angular spread (each user’s true bearing = nominal + Gaussian error σ from scattering/pointing error; N = 16, 20,000 draws per cell, seed 2024). Table = probability that SIR ≥ 10 dB:")
DF(m16,[3,3,3,3],{c:(lambda v:f"{v:.2f}") for c in m16.columns[1:]}|{"Separation (deg)":lambda v:int(v)})
BL(["<b>Why can’t two users in nearly the same direction be separated?</b> A uniform array resolves directions only through the phase progression ψ = π(sinθ<sub>A</sub> − sinθ<sub>B</sub>); the spatial signatures a(θ<sub>A</sub>) and a(θ<sub>B</sub>) become nearly parallel as Δ → 0 (|a<super>H</super>(θ<sub>A</sub>)a(θ<sub>B</sub>)|/N = |AF(Δ)/N| → 1). Any weight vector that passes A also passes B at almost the same gain: SIR = −20log|AF| → 0 dB (5° ⇒ 8.6 dB for N = 16 but only 0.4–1.8 dB for N = 4–8). Beamwidth ≈ 100°/N, so the resolvable separation is of the order of one beamwidth; more elements help only linearly in size, power and cost.",
"<b>Angular spread</b> (scattering around the user) fills the nulls and widens each user’s footprint: with σ = 5° only 55–70 % of 10°–15° separations still reach 10 dB (vs 100 % ideally), and at 3° separation the success is 0–24 %. A null placed exactly on B is fragile: for N = 16 a 1° bearing error turns a perfect null (−∞ dB) into only 18.5 dB of rejection, 2° into 14.3 dB (computed from the array factor), so real systems cannot count on deep nulls.",
"<b>Clustered users and other limits:</b> users grouped within a beamwidth cannot share a resource at all; the array needs direction/channel estimation (training overhead, mobility ⇒ tracking error at highway speeds); off-broadside steering widens the beam (HPBW 6.3° → 13° at 60°); more elements mean more RF chains and calibration; side lobes (−13 dB) limit the worst-case SIR; mixed LOS/NLOS makes ‘angle’ an ill-defined user label."])
P("<b>5.4 Design decision — toll plaza, most vehicles arriving from the same direction.</b> <b>I would not rely on SDMA there.</b> Cars queued on the approach road lie within a few degrees of each other: adjacent lanes of 3.5 m seen from 50 m are only ≈ 4° apart and vehicles in the same lane are at 0° (they differ in range, not bearing). Even N = 16 needs ≥ 5.3° at the ideal 10 dB threshold and, with just σ = 2° of angular spread, a 6° pair succeeds only 59 % of the time and a 3° pair 11 % of the time (table above). SDMA would give no reliable reuse where it is needed most. Use <b>time/frequency separation</b> (TDMA slots or OFDMA subcarriers, with the min-rate scheduler of Q4) among the clustered vehicles, and use arrays only to separate the <i>different approach directions</i> (inbound vs outbound roads, > 20° apart) or to improve link gain/interference rejection.")

