P("F. Defence of the final design (2 marks)",H2)
BL(["<b>P1 supported.</b> At N = 640 the original blocks 88.9 % of devices while the redesign blocks 1.4 % (Erlang-B 1.7 %); the 1 % point is ≈ 615 devices analytically and between 400 (0 %) and 640 (1.4 %) in simulation — <b>≈ 8× the 76 devices</b> (O1 met). At 800 devices blocking is 12 %.",
"<b>P2 supported.</b> Payload share of held time 10.3 % → 100 % (O2 met); channels are busy only while carrying a burst. At N = 640 the pool is 84 % busy with 84 % <i>payload</i> (original: 100 % busy with 10 % payload).",
"<b>P3 supported.</b> 2.9–3.1 messages per burst up to N = 1,000 (O3 met), rising to 3.4 at N = 1,500 from collisions. Mean access delay is 1.7 ms and p95 9.2 ms at N = 640 (≤ 50 ms met), 4.1 / 23 ms at 1,000.",
"<b>P4 supported.</b> T<sub>hold</sub> = 2 s halves the payload share (≈ 51 %), saves only ≈ 20 % of the messages (2.49 vs 3.09 per burst at N = 640) and raises blocking from 1.4 % to 42.7 % because idle-held channels occupy the pool. The hold timer is the wrong tool for ON/OFF with long OFF periods; it would pay off for talkspurt traffic where OFF ≲ T<sub>hold</sub> (PRMA voice).",
"<b>P5 partly supported.</b> At N = 1,500 the mean access delay jumps to 98 ms (p95 493 ms) — the RACH is saturating — while blocking (52 %) still follows Erlang-B (50 %). So the new bottleneck is the control channel, not the traffic channels; around N ≈ 1,000–1,500 the control channel needs more RACH slots or a reservation sub-frame.",
"<b>New costs and trade-offs:</b> signalling (≈ 3 messages per burst: at N = 640 ≈ 32 bursts/s ⇒ ≈ 100 messages/s on the control channel), access latency (ms instead of 0), more base-station state, probabilistic QoS (a burst may be rejected at overload — the original guarantees service once admitted), a control-channel capacity limit (≈ 80 requests/s here).",
"<b>When the original is still preferable:</b> (i) continuous/high duty traffic — at 90 % duty the on-demand design supports 68 devices vs 76 dedicated (gain 0.90×) and adds signalling for nothing (see duty table); (ii) ≤ 76 devices where guaranteed zero-latency access matters (voice calls, alarms); (iii) when blocking of a burst is unacceptable. The redesign’s advantage scales as 1/duty (8× at 10 %, 16× at 5 %, 40× at 2 %) — for the telemetry site of Q2 (0.12 % duty) the control channel, not the traffic channels, would then be the limit.",
"<b>Final engineering conclusion:</b> exploit the ON/OFF structure with burst-level on-demand allocation and T<sub>hold</sub> = 0; keep a few dedicated channels for continuous/guaranteed services; size the RACH to the burst arrival rate (capacity ≥ 2.7 × the request rate for slotted ALOHA)."])

